{
  "rows": [
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": null,
          "text": "indicating that a particular plan would have an effect contrary to the anticipated effect"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": null,
          "text": "claiming that a particular plan should not be adopted because, while effective, it would have at least one undesirable consequence"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": null,
          "text": "arguing that an alternative plan could achieve a desired result more easily than the plan originally proposed"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": null,
          "text": "questioning the assumption that authorities are available who have special knowledge of the problem under discussion"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": null,
          "text": "offering a counterexample in order to show that a particular general claim is too broadly stated"
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "7c19759a-73a1-4cb9-912f-4bdd38ddd9b1",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "The anticipated effect of Inez's plan was people to be *more* willing to buy antiques.\n\nWas Anika saying that Inez's plan would lead to people being *less* willing to buy antiques?\n\nSure. She doesn't explicitly say that, but Anika is saying that Inez's plan would force them to raise prices on their antiques.\n\nIs it common sense to think, \"if you raise the price on your antiques, people will be *less* willing to buy it?\"\n\nYes.\n\n(There might be a voice in your head saying, \"Not necessarily! Sometimes people are *more* attracted to buying something because the price is higher, like a Fendi bag, or like really expensive wine.\" Sure, but that's not the typical association. Businesses offer sales and discounts and coupons in order to create demand, in order to make people *more* willing to buy. They don't raise prices to do that, generally.)",
          "id": "62dff9ff-ef46-4351-a35d-57a338709212",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "6a178d21df9c2072211c203caa694020"
        },
        {
          "body": "This answer is wicked tempting because \"undesirable consequence\" is a great match for the Faulty Implications answer we were anticipating.\n\nAnika *did* claim that the plan has at least one undesirable consequence (raising prices).\n\nBut she never said the plan would be effective. Effectiveness is measured by whether the Plan achieves its Goal of \"making people more willing to buy antiques at the fair\".\n\nSince Anika says \"I disagree with your prediction\", she is saying \"I don't think the plan would be effective in making people more willing to buy antiques at the fair\".\n\nSo that part of the answer is contradicted by Anika's statements.",
          "id": "a2b452e4-f43b-4df5-99ac-e09fa277b55f",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "674817e6f98143a0e81ea26c2ef9ac65"
        },
        {
          "body": "Anika doesn't offer any alternative plan. She just points out problems with Inez's plan.",
          "id": "6101c0b8-4596-4c25-80e7-a4aca2cceb47",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "ea9abd156e5af45b0ec080015d23451e"
        },
        {
          "body": "Nothing Anika says ever touches on whether there would be appraisers available to judge the authenticity of these antiques.\n\nShe just says the appraisers are *unnecessary* (since the customers are already experts), not unavailable.",
          "id": "ef3f361b-c21b-4f0c-8259-083cfb7435be",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "20d811ab675ff7a3296daece954b5788"
        },
        {
          "body": "The only general claim Inez makes is that \"in poor economic times, people want to be sure they're getting good value for their money\".\n\nA counterexample to that would involve a specific case of poor economic times where someone *didn't* care about getting good value for their money.\n\nAnika doesn't present anything resembling that.",
          "id": "792089a5-5bc4-4d98-b3ac-5cb578ef4dcb",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "c8173ca0d202b5f7651a41e39b19b6ee"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT102-S4-Q19",
      "step_count": 6
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "asserting that the objection is based on a belief about the reliability of clairvoyance rather than on the nature of knowledge or its definition"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Contradicted",
          "text": "asserting that the case of clairvoyance is one of knowledge even though we do not really believe in clairvoyance as a reliable process"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Contradicted",
          "text": "arguing against the assumption that clairvoyance is unreliable"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Out of Scope: personal choice",
          "text": "explaining that the definition of knowledge is a matter of personal choice"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "\"Not a Case of \\\"Reliable Process\\\"\" [level=5]",
          "text": "demonstrating that the case of clairvoyance is not a case of knowledge and does not fit the definition of knowledge"
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "8228a2a1-40ed-4379-860a-589131d30159",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "Did the author say, the reason for the objection is our belief about the reliability of clairvoyance, not about this definition of knowledge?\n\nSure, the author said, I agree we would reject (object) to these claims, but we would do so **because** we really do not believe in clairvoyance as a reliable process.\n\nThe author asserted that we object because of our beliefs about the reliability of clairvoyance. The final sentence is elaborating that this definition is not the problem, because were we to think that clairvoyance were a reliable process, it would satisfy the definition and we would happily accept calling it knowledge.",
          "id": "d4bcf901-f958-4428-9ef8-6a139f9d089d",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "dc17dab9adfe0d8ba51c4da7b4546344"
        },
        {
          "body": "The author \"agrees that we would reject a clairvoyant person's claim to know certain things based on this power\".\n\nIn other words, \"You're right --- if a clairvoyant person claims to know XYZ via clairvoyance, we'd say you don't know XYZ.\"\n\nOur author thinks that clairvoyance is **not** a case of knowledge. He only thinks it would switch to being a case of knowledge if we were to switch into considering it a reliable process.",
          "id": "6b40461a-fd4c-4abb-ac51-19c1e70e3098",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "8c01a602349e39d048fe55ae28700a39"
        },
        {
          "body": "The author accepts (in a plural first person pronoun) that \" **we** really do not believe in clairvoyance as a reliable process\".\n\nHe's not arguing against that assumption. He accepts the assumption that clairvoyance is unreliable.",
          "id": "995959e9-57a0-4fd7-8aa8-f77db04044c7",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "547f3912ae0265d0e996a6d7aa8909bc"
        },
        {
          "body": "The author never says the definition is a matter of personal choice. The author is defending this definition against an objection, saying that a supposed counterexample isn't really a counterexample.\n\nBut he never explains that \"how one defines knowledge is really up to them\".",
          "id": "0e0a74fe-d567-43ec-ba5b-288bacb1160f",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "4bfbb2c186b919f3e821d8de96d4be26"
        },
        {
          "body": "So, so close. The author demonstrates that,\n\nthe case of clairvoyance is not a case of **reliable process** and thus does not fit the definition of knowledge.\n\nThe author does acknowledge that clairvoyance is not a case of knowledge, but that isn't part of defending the definition. The defense of the definition comes when the author says, \"the reason clairvoyance isn't knowledge is because of **reliable process**, not because of **bad definition**.\"",
          "id": "fe56b0ec-4aae-4fc8-b5d1-2aec0d1ce2bd",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "57ba4083d832eb6136967506732cbf5d"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT112-S3-Q8",
      "step_count": 5
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "Fewer diseases attack leatherbacks than attack other large aquatic reptiles."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "The average life expectancy of sea turtles in general is longer than that of almost all other marine animals."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "Most leatherbacks that perish in the wild are killed by predators."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Restates Paradox",
          "text": "Few zoologists have sufficient knowledge to establish an artificial environment that is conducive to the well-being of captive leatherbacks."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "The size of a leatherback is an untrustworthy indicator of its age."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "4e0d31b1-37db-42db-a39a-40e976a4732d",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "A compares leatherbacks with other large aquatic reptiles. The surprise is a comparison between captive leatherbacks and wild ones. Fewer diseases attacking leatherbacks would be true in both places, so it creates no difference between captivity and the wild that could offset the captivity diseases.",
          "id": "95b813da-ad0e-4942-9f81-60b5d11defc3",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "d7187b0821460de4da69e6d576954cd7"
        },
        {
          "body": "B compares sea turtles with other marine animals, which is the wrong comparison. Even if sea turtles live long in general, that is true of captive and wild leatherbacks alike, so it doesn't explain why the extra diseases in captivity fail to lower the captive odds.",
          "id": "1bf3ba6b-8337-4212-bff8-746ce5757312",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "9dd92a5a8ac787b5841c66444be48e0b"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the something-bad-about-the-wild answer we predicted. Captive leatherbacks face diseases the wild ones never meet. Wild leatherbacks face predators, and C tells us predators kill most of the wild ones that perish. Captive turtles are presumably safe from predators. So each setting has its own main danger, and the odds of reaching full life expectancy can come out about the same.\n\nThe move to repeat: when two groups end up equal even though one has an obvious disadvantage, look for a disadvantage on the other side that balances it.",
          "id": "86782073-ac68-4ac1-9494-90dac1b164f1",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "04dc9a0f1171bd3ce070af08ded85cc7"
        },
        {
          "body": "D deepens the paradox. If few zoologists know how to build an environment that is good for captive leatherbacks, captivity sounds even worse for them. That is one more reason to expect captive turtles to die younger, which is the opposite of what we need.",
          "id": "6629b443-0328-4197-ab72-cbc19c170594",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "15625679fc09774954bbf6ba38f9668d"
        },
        {
          "body": "E might tempt you if you suspect the life-expectancy figures are measured badly. But nothing in E says the error affects captive and wild turtles differently. If size is a poor guide to age everywhere, it gives no difference between the two settings, so the surprise remains.",
          "id": "a6695012-35bf-4641-b9ab-e8501b6b6f52",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "af41ea1cb15cd5038fffaf5984bfe0b2"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT117-S3-Q2",
      "step_count": 4
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "Fewer diseases attack leatherbacks than attack other large aquatic reptiles."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "The average life expectancy of sea turtles in general is longer than that of almost all other marine animals."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "Most leatherbacks that perish in the wild are killed by predators."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Restates Paradox",
          "text": "Few zoologists have sufficient knowledge to establish an artificial environment that is conducive to the well-being of captive leatherbacks."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "The size of a leatherback is an untrustworthy indicator of its age."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "50518ed3-43a3-4ed7-bfd5-471e6a2ecdf2",
      "notes": null,
      "qid": "PT117-S3-Q2",
      "step_count": 4
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "Fewer diseases attack leatherbacks than attack other large aquatic reptiles."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "The average life expectancy of sea turtles in general is longer than that of almost all other marine animals."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "Most leatherbacks that perish in the wild are killed by predators."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Restates Paradox",
          "text": "Few zoologists have sufficient knowledge to establish an artificial environment that is conducive to the well-being of captive leatherbacks."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "No Distinction",
          "text": "The size of a leatherback is an untrustworthy indicator of its age."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "1db57b15-062b-45c4-8bba-2e28f16ea7db",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "This compares leatherbacks to other reptiles. The paradox is internal to leatherbacks — captive vs. wild. Whatever leatherbacks face relative to other species doesn't change why captive and wild leatherbacks happen to have similar survival rates despite their different threat environments.",
          "id": "b0f4c619-39c6-426c-be21-f43bbfdfdafb",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "47a86d62eee82b3142c4f4b7f0828cb0"
        },
        {
          "body": "This says sea turtles outlive most other marine animals. Cool fact, but the paradox is about why leatherbacks have similar life-expectancy odds in captivity and in the wild — within the species. Comparisons to other animals don't address that within-species puzzle.",
          "id": "699ac422-f4e8-45ac-802c-691a4c8203cf",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "d84d587abf38396e5bd6cfd037839c2c"
        },
        {
          "body": "This balances the scales. Captivity has its own threat — fatal diseases that don't exist in the wild. If captives faced that and wild ones faced nothing, captives would die younger on average. But this answer tells us wild leatherbacks face their own deadly threat: predators. Captive leatherbacks don't deal with predators at all.\n\nSo both environments have a major mortality factor — disease in captivity, predators in the wild — and they cancel out, producing similar survival odds in both. That's exactly the kind of answer that resolves a \"should be different but isn't\" paradox.",
          "id": "8048e811-07c5-4dac-97ed-28417d32644a",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "dbbdcae6b1006c2399cf2d9d58e8e809"
        },
        {
          "body": "This makes the paradox worse, not better. If zoologists struggle to give captive leatherbacks a good environment, captives would be expected to do *even worse* than wild ones — yet they don't. The puzzle becomes more puzzling, not less. We need something that explains why the survival rates come out even, not something that should make them more uneven.",
          "id": "465a6f63-8409-4b4e-a60d-f1c3aa2903cc",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "edd90804f6fd5b423582da714c36602c"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is about whether you can tell a leatherback's age from its size. That's a research methodology issue. The paradox is about actual survival outcomes — how often each group reaches its maximum life expectancy. The age-indicator question doesn't affect those underlying outcomes.",
          "id": "df98fce5-7396-4580-aa28-81da471a3d02",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "33321e57c9f888a5ffa9191e03771b01"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT117-S3-Q2",
      "step_count": 4
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "It makes an appeal to a general principle."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It denies the good faith of an opponent."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It relies on evaluating the predictable consequences of a proposal."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It substitutes description for giving a rationale for a policy."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It employs a term on two different occasions in different senses."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "a0084eb1-010f-486a-9dd2-51acfc8f4d80",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "Rossi's first sentence is a general rule: it is undemocratic for anyone to live under a government that doesn't represent their interests. Rossi then applies it to one group. Children's interests sometimes differ from their parents', so, by the rule, children need their own representation, which means the vote.\n\n\"Appeal to a general principle\" is how the LSAT describes this move. When an argument opens with a broad \"it is wrong / undemocratic / unfair to ...\" claim and then applies it to a specific case, look for this answer.",
          "id": "5f94de0b-c9e8-424d-ab16-ebba4e5e1658",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "eef186f32f17cbe3c154a23c14aeb9fe"
        },
        {
          "body": "Rossi speaks first, so there is no opponent yet, and Rossi never questions anyone's honesty or motives. Smith doesn't either: Smith grants Rossi's point about interests and disagrees on the merits. An answer about bad faith needs an accusation in the text, and there is none.",
          "id": "42536bcf-8259-468e-8e07-74eea53c32b6",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "1abc39b1c140b6f5faf316f1e169dd27"
        },
        {
          "body": "Rossi's argument is about what is democratic, not about what would happen if children voted. Rossi never predicts a single result. Smith's worry that children's conceptions are too simple comes closer to practical concerns, which is why C can feel familiar: it fits the other speaker better.",
          "id": "31f8e41a-ef98-4e07-ad56-220638f59925",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "607d0e055372da8270c6ab51d3c78b7c"
        },
        {
          "body": "D can tempt because Rossi's argument is short and sounds like a pair of statements. But Rossi does give a rationale. The policy is \"children should have the right to vote.\" The rationale is the principle (living with unrepresented interests is undemocratic) plus the fact (children's interests sometimes differ from their parents'). Test it: ask Rossi \"why should children vote?\" Rossi has an answer. An argument that substituted description for a rationale would only describe children or voting and never say why the policy follows.",
          "id": "027271a5-c2dd-4155-9166-90f07b61e9bc",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "e98c4856b9963a84823316f19c73081c"
        },
        {
          "body": "This describes equivocation: one word used in two senses. The only term Rossi repeats is \"interests,\" and it means the same thing both times: what is good for a person or group. With no shift in meaning, there is no equivocation.",
          "id": "475f1a9c-a62a-4af2-869f-032a3e397a54",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "6ecea033097e0a0d7ec7f6f1306063e2"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT117-S3-Q8",
      "step_count": 4
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "It makes an appeal to a general principle."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
