        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "a96f3c58-d586-4768-ac69-73d1b91756f2",
      "notes": null,
      "qid": "PT117-S3-Q8",
      "step_count": 2
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "It makes an appeal to a general principle."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It denies the good faith of an opponent."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It relies on evaluating the predictable consequences of a proposal."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It substitutes description for giving a rationale for a policy."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It employs a term on two different occasions in different senses."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "6915a663-a557-4f9c-88ec-40c22400917d",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "This is a clean description of Rossi's move. The argument starts with a broad democratic principle (governments need to represent the interests of those they govern) and then shows it applies to a specific group (children with distinct interests). That's textbook appeal to a general principle.",
          "id": "af02b9e3-ca5b-4858-8d23-4e75ffabf733",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "6403ba25d0d637889fbb70829cf64b72"
        },
        {
          "body": "Rossi doesn't attack anyone's honesty or sincerity. Rossi just makes a positive case from a general principle. There's no opponent being accused of bad faith.",
          "id": "13893cc7-8132-492e-b945-c63dd667851f",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "67bdab7f67ce9e05386e00b8fb565eac"
        },
        {
          "body": "This describes a consequentialist argument — weighing predicted outcomes. Rossi doesn't do that. Rossi argues from principle (representation is owed to those governed) to conclusion (children should vote). The consequence-based reasoning belongs to Smith, not Rossi.",
          "id": "56c683a5-0cd3-4d88-8d7a-d61764c9e9d4",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "94d81aed61fccb55b83a4c2f88febd4a"
        },
        {
          "body": "This accuses Rossi of just describing the situation without giving a reason. But Rossi gives a clear reason — the democratic principle of representation. There's a real argument here, not just description.",
          "id": "24b8f0a7-259a-4508-875a-cf23a6af7e3e",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "c3e1695e476e9ca27774fcb925c6fec9"
        },
        {
          "body": "Rossi doesn't use a word with two different meanings. The argument moves cleanly from principle to application without any term getting redefined along the way. So this isn't a sleight-of-hand argument.",
          "id": "58293fc1-4d16-4f9a-a736-e725f4381999",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "66a643fb61e286def25e2143b6626644"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT117-S3-Q8",
      "step_count": 3
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Unsupported",
          "text": "The number of amateur musicians who play popular music has decreased."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Unsupported",
          "text": "Most professional musicians are able to play both electric and nonelectric instruments."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Unsupported",
          "text": "The number of professional musicians in some bands has increased."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "The total number of professional bands has increased as a result of electrification."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Unsupported",
          "text": "Many professional musicians play in more than one band."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "776ae5b7-d64d-4a29-b77c-acbc385dd38d",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "The paragraph only counts musicians who play popular music professionally. Amateurs never come up, so we have no information about whether their numbers rose, fell, or stayed the same.",
          "id": "7354eaa5-1294-49df-ab0f-3458e9be7389",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "76227f061b8954b11bd70e37e0eb6cd9"
        },
        {
          "body": "The paragraph says instruments were electrified. It never says whether any musician can play a nonelectric instrument, let alone most of them. A claim about musicians' skills has nothing to stand on here.",
          "id": "962b0b9d-9835-47ba-9e0a-02e0921495bc",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "1a51abc82af02df567c842462a01ef01"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the most popular wrong answer. The *average* band got smaller. Some bands could have grown, but nothing in the passage says so, and the average would also fall if every single band shrank. An answer that is merely possible isn't supported.",
          "id": "f87da5d7-ee95-47ab-a6b8-8040d21a3621",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "19ad242b58d63830ce5428fbe665b77f"
        },
        {
          "body": "Fewer musicians per band, more musicians overall. The only way both are true is more bands. Picture 100 musicians in bands of 5: 20 bands. Now 150 musicians in bands of 3: 50 bands. Electrification drives both trends, so crediting it with the extra bands is fair.",
          "id": "c6105cdb-0554-4fc3-9def-e6cf49374678",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "7ca2d295634ef1fcab5a24332c37237f"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is one way to spread musicians across more bands, but nothing in the paragraph says anyone plays in more than one band. The math only forces more bands; it does not tell us how musicians are spread across them. Every musician could play in exactly one band and the facts still hold.",
          "id": "d91af5c4-50d1-42f6-a8d5-9de7ed44243e",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "33d8856c85ac78df919bea33af524594"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT117-S4-Q3",
      "step_count": 3
    },
    {
