      "step_count": 3
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Out of Scope",
          "text": "The National Tea Association has announced that it plans to carry out its own retail survey in the next year."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "No Impact",
          "text": "A survey by an unrelated polling organization shows that the public is generally receptive to the idea of trying new types of tea."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "No Impact",
          "text": "The Survey Group is funded by a consortium of consumer advocacy groups."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "The stores from which the Survey Group collected information about tea sales are all located in the same small region of the country."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "No Impact",
          "text": "Tea has been the subject of an expensive and efficient advertising campaign funded, in part, by the National Tea Association."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "990b6e7f-6fdd-4119-8d12-d4b060edd914",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "A survey the Association only plans to run next year gives us no data now. Announcing a survey isn't evidence of anything, and it doesn't touch the Survey Group's 20 years of flat sales. Even if the new survey comes out differently, A doesn't tell us that.",
          "id": "289a4c15-33eb-490e-8a63-246409dd1bb3",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "55f202d8b016569c45a8af039bfd9a24"
        },
        {
          "body": "Being \"generally receptive to the idea of trying new types of tea\" is an attitude, not a purchase, and it isn't a change over time. The public could have been just as receptive 20 years ago. Barr's conclusion is about tea being more popular now than before, and B gives no before-and-after comparison.",
          "id": "68183fc3-4093-4c1f-9ba6-751848661e58",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "998f6fc297cf0a2a638366d8dc4c50e1"
        },
        {
          "body": "C is trying to make us doubt the Survey Group. But why would consumer advocacy groups want tea sales to look flat? C gives no motive, and the stimulus calls the Survey Group neutral. Funding alone, with no reason to skew the data, doesn't weaken the survey.",
          "id": "c0fb0987-0d69-4438-a800-f3233a7b7e7d",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "43db90f5d3c35d93348458cd67f3d54b"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the excuse for the survey data we predicted. If every surveyed store is \"located in the same small region of the country,\" flat sales there tell us very little about the country as a whole. Tea could be booming everywhere else.\n\nThe move to repeat: when the evidence is a survey or a sample, weaken by showing the sample doesn't represent the group the conclusion is about.",
          "id": "132f3d4a-b8a5-4d21-94ed-1c6dea6bb1a7",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "7eb8cc783164be36a613237de29f02de"
        },
        {
          "body": "E is the tempting one because an \"expensive and efficient advertising campaign\" sounds like it should boost popularity. But it tells us about effort, not results, and Barr's data says sales didn't move. If anything, E helps Barr: it explains the visibility the Association cited as paid advertising, so that visibility is weaker evidence that people actually like tea more.",
          "id": "8fa4ece0-ad52-4651-a9ac-1a8a959d82e4",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "4ba52d5900d6ed683bbd8f38492b17ac"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT117-S4-Q5",
      "step_count": 4
    },
