      "explanation_id": "9f047239-a8fb-4770-a006-05917b3bb6d9",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "The Tea Association announcing it will do a survey next year is just news about a future event. It tells us nothing about whether tea is more popular now or whether the Survey Group's data is reliable. To weaken Barr we need something that calls his evidence into question, not something that mentions the other side.",
          "id": "86156b23-150e-4428-8276-bbabe0f231ed",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "5612c12996f18deff5ab0dd40ad6a1ad"
        },
        {
          "body": "This sounds like it might help, but watch the gap. The public being \"receptive to the idea of trying\" new teas is a measure of attitude, not behavior. Barr's argument is built on actual sales numbers. People can say they are open to something and still never buy it. So this answer does not actually contradict Barr's evidence.",
          "id": "3984e0f8-1b8e-458a-b8a1-a7907801dca9",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "5c1f0e52cc42be8d419c273e2d0545aa"
        },
        {
          "body": "This might feel like a \"the data is biased\" answer, but it is not. The stimulus already says the Survey Group is neutral, and the answer does not actually allege bias — it just names the funder. Without a story about how consumer advocacy funding would distort tea sales numbers, this leaves Barr's evidence intact.",
          "id": "023118db-a547-4c0e-8ebe-a894e66913c4",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "e64a5a291b982699c1801708def4b40d"
        },
        {
          "body": "Here is the weakener. Barr's entire case rides on the Survey Group's sales data. If those stores are all in one tiny corner of the country, then \"no change in tea sales\" only tells us about that corner — not about the country as a whole.\n\nIt is like trying to figure out whether a band is getting more popular nationwide by checking ticket sales at one venue in one city. Even if those numbers are flat, the band could be exploding everywhere else.\n\nThe Tea Association's claim that tea is getting more popular nationally could easily still be right; Barr just has not actually disproved it.",
          "id": "ec90f645-36ea-414a-8908-f890d93e8742",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "479de8108d03ba6d47945d168fc00f0d"
        },
        {
          "body": "A big ad campaign is the kind of thing the Tea Association would point to in its argument that tea is getting more popular — but advertising is effort, not result. Barr's whole point is that the sales numbers stayed flat *despite* all that visibility. Telling us the ad campaign was big and efficient does not show sales actually moved.",
          "id": "e7b168ea-477b-44ef-a5f5-82dc48f39263",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "11b178ca1c8630e0408882dcd06c17c9"
        }
      ],
