      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "Fatima is a mathematician confronted by the vastness of what nobody has solved. The saying is about realizing how much you have to know before you can grasp how little you know. That is actually a reasonable match — the story illustrates the proverb. So this usage is fine, not inappropriate.",
          "id": "9a7b15e5-3561-4442-a9e7-7cd6f060783a",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "41af42e4da2e6f025d7931c1eeb19bc4"
        },
        {
          "body": "Harold's wallet-forgetting trick is something he has done many times. The saying treats selfishness as predictable — like gravity. The story matches the saying: a reliable, predictable instance of selfishness. Used appropriately.",
          "id": "e345adf8-a7bb-4409-9fd3-19852ed62ffe",
