    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Bad Match",
          "text": "National resources should be regulated by international agreement when wildlife is endangered."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Opposite",
          "text": "The right of future generations to have wildlife preserved supersedes the economic needs of individual nations."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "Only when a reduction of populations of endangered species by commercial development has been found should regulation be implemented to prevent further damage."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Opposite",
          "text": "Environment regulation must aim at preventing any further environmental damage and cannot allow for the different degrees to which different nations have already harmed the environment."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Opposite",
          "text": "It is imprudent to allow further depletion of natural resources."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "22045154-79ec-4a5a-a196-5b1030ef9c60",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "The Development Commission claims Figoria has \"as much right to govern our own resources\" as other countries. Handing regulation to an international agreement takes that right away, and the trigger, endangered wildlife, is the Wildlife Commission's whole case. A works against the side it is supposed to help.",
          "id": "9414f535-1f49-4717-afca-39a9bf5dfa61",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "dc8abda206dcf46eca6ca8ccbe383efb"
        },
        {
          "body": "The Development Commission's case rests partly on growth, which is an economic need. B says the right of future generations to preserved wildlife outranks \"the economic needs of individual nations,\" so that appeal loses. This is a principle the Wildlife Commission would cite, not the Development Commission.",
          "id": "ee10280a-95db-45de-9227-c415df0e66f9",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "a962ce67f210b22e15064ae12c959ff5"
        },
        {
          "body": "C says regulation should happen only when a reduction of endangered species by commercial development \"has been found.\" Flip that around: if no such reduction has been found, no regulation yet. The Development Commission says Figoria has conserved and that its development \"might not affect wildlife,\" so no harm has been found in Figoria. The Wildlife Commission wants regulation before any harm, based on damage in other nations. C rules that out.\n\nThat is the move when a principle question asks you to support one side against the other: find the exact point where the two sides split, then pick the principle whose condition the favored side's evidence satisfies.",
          "id": "5e584a1f-0c0e-41e9-832c-52d3d03ba4b9",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "ca5b164a4037b36906c903f2e4c29d11"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the trap. It talks about how different nations have harmed the environment to different degrees. That's the Development Commission's theme, so it feels like their answer. But read what it concludes: regulation cannot allow for those differences. The commission's whole complaint is that Figoria's record of conserving should count. D says it doesn't.",
          "id": "9cf1246a-6905-4908-8777-e7807a6169a6",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "68476c65f0898d820408137c728bc4b0"
        },
        {
          "body": "Developing wetlands depletes a natural resource. If further depletion is \"imprudent,\" the development the Development Commission is asking for is imprudent too. E gives the Wildlife Commission a reason to restrict development, which is the opposite of what we need.",
          "id": "ddeec7cd-5d0a-4b00-bb0d-5ab449aeedbe",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "4c830c575e0d642a47d05446d62c6231"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT118-S3-Q8",
      "step_count": 6
    },
