      "explanation_id": "c85b4733-212a-4157-a263-721f4de92380",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "Watch the word \"required.\" That word means you cannot get adequate deep sleep without afternoon exercise. The study only showed that afternoon exercisers got *more* deep sleep than non-exercisers — it never said the non-exercisers were sleep-deprived. Plenty of people without afternoon exercise routines presumably sleep just fine.",
          "id": "ab345b6e-0f98-4fe3-b052-f4f3608c1794",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "d3863714028433bf9e12d14b5fc620b9"
        },
        {
          "body": "The mechanism specifically depends on the temperature boost lasting until bedtime. Morning exercise might wear off long before then. Since the stimulus says nothing about morning exercise, we cannot conclude it is almost as effective as afternoon exercise.",
          "id": "4c20fa23-10e6-46c4-be88-7f7e13d11b2a",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "2d01d7a067798890e13d01549afb3d5e"
        },
        {
          "body": "Notice \"the best way.\" For something to be the best, you would have to know about all the alternatives and rule them out. The stimulus only describes one mechanism that works. There could be other methods — sleep medications, cool rooms, blackout curtains — that work even better. We have no evidence to rank them.",
          "id": "df0dceb3-3874-4390-bd74-6c5a513acd2f",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "b74e85571ffd82608b47e16263161990"
        },
        {
          "body": "\"No one\" is a sweeping claim about every single person in the control group. The stimulus only tells us about averages. Some control-group members could easily have had a body-temperature bump from a warm meal, a hot bath, or even being a little sick. The data does not exclude that.",
          "id": "a8ef1ce6-68de-4a53-879a-60a91234a13f",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "b449acc39a4c7fe06897b59de7e65fe8"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the answer that respects the mechanism. The researcher said the deep sleep comes from the slight body-temperature rise that lasts until past bedtime. So what really matters is the temperature, not the exercise.\n\nA warm bath right before bed produces exactly that condition: a small temperature boost going into bedtime. So we would expect a similar effect on deep sleep.\n\nNotice the hedged language too — \"will likely result.\" That matches what the stimulus actually supports. No overreach.",
          "id": "af27ec17-224a-4094-8e6e-289c3201c1fb",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "d7ea56784ba2c7059b88e270fd353b07"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT119-S2-Q12",
      "step_count": 3
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It makes a generalization about Egyptian society based on a sample so small that it is likely to be unrepresentative."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Equivocation",
          "text": "It uses the term “alcoholic beverage” in a different sense in the premises than in the conclusion."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It presumes, without providing justification, that because one society developed a technology before another, the development in the latter was dependent on the development in the former."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "It ignores the possibility that the first known instance of a kind is not the first instance of that kind."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "It provides no evidence for the claim that the Babylonians produced wine as early as 1500 B.C."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "530b861d-8d2a-4e8d-8a83-b394c89148d4",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "A generalization flaw would need a claim about Egyptian society as a whole, such as how common beer drinking was. The conclusion isn't about that. One cup is enough to show alcohol was being made in Egypt by 2000 B.C. The real question is whether anyone made it before then.",
          "id": "88fa80c1-6a8d-4f41-a4e2-06bc9b93e03e",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "97e68f5a5da994089cd1068769812fe0"
        },
        {
          "body": "The term holds steady. The premises mention Babylonian wine and Egyptian beer, and the conclusion's \"alcoholic beverages\" covers both in the same sense. Nothing shifts meaning between premises and conclusion. The argument compares dates, so the weak spot is about what came first, not what the words mean.",
          "id": "f0375ec8-e3e1-4fef-ba3d-2bfba1f62a19",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "2a14f6e9420b9d2fff14e5740e51b259"
        },
        {
          "body": "The argument never says Babylonian wine-making depended on Egyptian brewing. It claims only that Egypt came first in time. C describes a claim about one society's technology depending on another's, and the author makes no such claim, so there is nothing for this criticism to attack.",
          "id": "3de5d70d-4fdc-4b17-9043-13b657a42b57",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "6f374644ada929cff1062e8cab48c5b0"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the gap we predicted. \"The first known instance of a kind\" is the 2000 B.C. Egyptian cup, the oldest evidence found so far. \"The first instance of that kind\" is what the conclusion claims: the Egyptians were the first society ever to produce alcohol. The author treats the two as the same. Test it: could some society have made alcohol before 2000 B.C. without leaving evidence we have found? Yes, and the Babylonians losing their title shows new finds can overturn an old first. When a Flaw conclusion claims a first, look for this answer.",
          "id": "4a8201c9-e781-4de8-94ba-6bcce841130c",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "8cb8a3f1aa02bb41f3a991c44694c693"
        },
        {
          "body": "Arguments may state background facts without proving them, and the 1500 B.C. date mainly tells us what used to be believed. Supporting it wouldn't help the argument anyway: even a well-documented Babylonian date can't show that no society produced alcohol before 2000 B.C. The flaw is elsewhere.",
          "id": "81ca6f1f-1b28-4e5e-a272-c34f0e0e98a1",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "9fd2337935a91cdf9c88b9dc3eb0c486"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT119-S2-Q24",
      "step_count": 4
    },
