      "explanation_id": "3ec2fe76-af84-45df-a0ed-9a8aefa65283",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "Sampling flaws happen when you draw a broad conclusion about a population from too few examples. Here the argument is not generalizing about Egyptian society — it is making a single comparative claim about who came first. The cup is one piece of evidence, not a sample. This is naming a flaw the argument does not commit.",
          "id": "7e11fe85-3482-41a8-a36e-4473924f63bf",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "9a17058be5cf5a0f55d236f6e8a9dd0f"
        },
        {
          "body": "Equivocation means using the same word in two different senses. Here \"alcoholic beverage\" covers both Babylonian wine and Egyptian beer in the same sense. There is no slippery word swap between evidence and conclusion. Wrong flaw.",
          "id": "4ab4411b-2bb7-4a0e-ab9c-fba62f55bfb2",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "714847dbacc5cdf3dc6ef4e089dc2c2a"
        },
        {
          "body": "This describes an \"earlier developer caused the later developer\" flaw. The argument never says the Babylonians got fermentation from the Egyptians — only that the Egyptians did it first. There is no claim of causation or dependence to attack. Wrong flaw.",
          "id": "dc8d266f-ddb0-42ad-8ba0-1ba50e609f8b",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "af2cc41d9c546941aba7634367aa3c2e"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the right diagnosis. The author found the oldest piece of evidence we have for alcohol production. That is not the same as showing Egyptians were the first ever.\n\nThink of it this way: the oldest written document we have ever discovered is not necessarily the very first document anyone ever wrote. Maybe earlier ones existed and got destroyed, or maybe we just have not found them yet. The same problem applies here.\n\nThe argument leaps from \"oldest evidence we have\" to \"first ever.\" That is the flaw, and this answer names it precisely.",
          "id": "dc5de37a-34b6-4bea-b00e-e5e610008d1b",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "1f6fe051e403bbc6451fb29f0d945e92"
        },
        {
          "body": "The Babylonian-wine claim is the conventional view the argument is replacing. Even if we just accept it on faith, the argument still works as written: 2000 B.C. is older than 1500 B.C. So failing to provide evidence for the Babylonian date is not the actual flaw — and it is also not really a flaw, since the argument is treating that as background.",
          "id": "2163e59a-3a7e-4ebe-ad49-02acc8ddca0e",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "5a20426dedf738cd9f2bbed97583540f"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT119-S2-Q24",
      "step_count": 3
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Unsupported",
          "text": "The behavior of organisms with elaborate brain mechanisms is usually not instinctual."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "Insect behavior is exclusively instinctual."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Too Strong",
          "text": "All organisms with brains larger than insects’ brains are capable of some measure of flexible behavior."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Too Strong",
          "text": "All organisms with large brains are biologically equipped for flexible behavior."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Too Strong",
          "text": "Only organisms with brains of insect size or smaller engage in purely instinctual behavior."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "a343e345-371c-419c-bee8-15c49d6371ff",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "\"Usually not instinctual\" is a claim about how organisms with elaborate brains actually behave. The stimulus gives a requirement for flexible behavior, so it can only tell us who can't behave flexibly. An organism with plenty of neurons could still run on instinct most of the time without breaking any statement.",
          "id": "db4dc1df-5fab-4cd9-b137-b0d1cb3ef2c2",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "7cac87c4035cc977f735d4262f221513"
        },
        {
          "body": "Run the chain from insects. No insect brain provides a sufficiently large number of neurons, and flexible behavior requires one, so no insect brain is capable of flexible behavior. The stimulus defines flexible as noninstinctual, so whatever insects do is instinctual.\n\n\"Exclusively\" sounds strong, but it's earned: the requirement is missing for every insect, so there is no room for even a little flexible behavior. When a strong word shows up on Must Be True, check whether a universal rule (here, \"no insect brain\") backs it before crossing it off.",
          "id": "fde33acb-dc48-4914-ae5d-4ea0b6e14c71",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "8b5c456653937abe1df846ef7b8b146c"
        },
        {
          "body": "Larger than an insect's brain isn't the same as large enough. The stimulus only says insect brains fall short. A brain could beat an insect's and still lack the neurons. And even a brain with enough neurons isn't guaranteed flexible behavior, because the neuron count is a requirement, not a guarantee.",
          "id": "06296952-c65d-422b-814b-1714860093e6",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "650be0ec3c30e277395752f50c43df62"
        },
        {
          "body": "This reverses the rule. Capable of flexible behavior → large number of neurons says the neurons are required. D reads it as large brain → equipped for flexible behavior, treating the requirement as enough. Driving legally requires a license, but holding a license doesn't make someone a driver. D also swaps \"large brain\" for \"large number of neurons,\" which the stimulus never equates.",
          "id": "a804f0e2-9d72-492b-9f50-465b9ca0598b",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "5cffdab2d455724dcd714a5ae3b69bc2"
        },
        {
          "body": "E says purely instinctual → brain of insect size or smaller. We only know the other direction: insect → instinctual. An organism with a bigger brain that still falls short of the neuron count would also be limited to instinct, and nothing rules that out. \"Only\" makes a small brain a requirement for pure instinct, and the stimulus never sets that requirement.",
          "id": "364af33b-7f64-43bb-86c8-340bfd18bdc1",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "85420ad86bb54c3999c93a2499af3b62"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT119-S2-Q7",
      "step_count": 3
    },
    {
