      "explanation_id": "a0224ca3-0b3a-4b7d-9c03-d4830eb7fbc9",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "This claims that elaborate-brain organisms *usually* behave non-instinctually. The passage tells us they have the capacity for flexible behavior, but it never says how often they use it. They could spend most of their time on instinct and only occasionally use flexibility. The \"usually\" is unsupported.",
          "id": "fd8bc070-fde4-4a38-93ff-171da06fb83b",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "c7c368ff7f645f790d9c29778ee93571"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is exactly the inference the chain forces. The passage gives a clean two-option dichotomy: behavior is instinctual or flexible (noninstinctual). Flexible behavior needs many neurons. Insect brains don't have many.\n\nSo insects can't do flexible behavior. The only kind they can do is instinctual. That makes their behavior exclusively instinctual — which is exactly what (B) says.",
          "id": "90a84be6-39c0-4c1b-be15-a6958d0cb8f9",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "06c56d52ba9cdfb4ff8369bc97cb990e"
        },
        {
          "body": "The passage tells us insect brains fall below the neuron threshold for flexible behavior. It says nothing about whether *every* non-insect organism clears that threshold. Plenty of small animals might still have brains too small for flexibility. \"All organisms with brains larger than insects'\" is much too sweeping.",
          "id": "11c4cc85-d8f2-430a-99de-e0c856262f27",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "a86c6bc18ba7372269f35ee56ef457ab"
        },
        {
          "body": "The passage requires \"a large number of neurons\" for flexible behavior. This answer talks about \"large brains,\" which isn't quite the same — different organisms pack neurons differently, and not every large-brained organism has the right kind of neuron density. The passage also doesn't commit to \"all\" large-brained organisms being capable.",
          "id": "67df7c0f-9551-47b6-a40d-8dce2a9091d9",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "d6e66610cfcab7cf41865cb6947bed6e"
        },
        {
          "body": "This answer says only insect-or-smaller-brained organisms do purely instinctual behavior. The passage tells us insects fall below the neuron threshold, so they're instinct-only. But it doesn't say no other animal does. There could be non-insect animals also limited to instinct. The \"only\" is too strong.",
          "id": "fb38346b-4557-4ded-bf32-28c6d0735b89",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "1be0d098bada414c6f29ab88988871f7"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT119-S2-Q7",
      "step_count": 4
    },
    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Too Strong",
          "text": "The manipulation of the genetic code of organisms is never unethical."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Bad Assumption",
          "text": "Anything that is accomplished by nature is not unethical to accomplish with science."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "The manipulation of the genetic code through selective breeding for desired traits is not unethical."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Out of Scope",
          "text": "The manipulation of the genetic code through selective breeding for desired traits is important for human survival."
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Too Strong",
          "text": "Science can accomplish only what is already in some sense natural, and nothing natural is unethical."
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "3dbbe7ec-5548-455f-b9e4-fa006c62eaf5",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "\"Never\" is far more than the argument needs. The scientist's evidence is about one kind of manipulation, selective breeding. Negate A: some manipulation of genetic codes is unethical, say, manipulation done to cause harm. The scientist can accept that and still argue that genetic engineering, like selective breeding, is not unethical.",
          "id": "bbc83bd7-a43f-448d-8303-87715ee990a2",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "68b52013bcc5272cc65649707f16bb8b"
        },
        {
          "body": "B is tempting because it sounds like a defense of genetic engineering. But the scientist's evidence is selective breeding, something people did, not something nature accomplished. Negate B: some things nature accomplishes would be unethical to accomplish with science. The comparison between breeding and genetic engineering is untouched.",
          "id": "24dabea0-eb2c-4347-b6c6-43e836b49b17",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "8c3c7ee9e744243cba7fae79a7400ef5"
        },
        {
          "body": "Negate it: \"selective breeding for desired traits is unethical.\" Then the analogy turns against the scientist. Showing that genetic engineering is like selective breeding only puts it next to something unethical, and the millennia of practice just mean we've been doing something wrong for a long time. The conclusion falls.\n\nThe move: in an argument by analogy, the conclusion borrows a property from the comparison case. The argument has to assume the comparison case actually has that property.",
          "id": "9d05888d-1674-455d-9d01-37cd5e465428",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "d51f4a300bdb0a82665f3ae1af84ab87"
        },
        {
          "body": "The argument is about ethics, not usefulness. Negate D: selective breeding is not important for human survival. Breeding could still be ethical, and the analogy still works. Something can be unimportant and perfectly acceptable.",
          "id": "710820c3-cac8-4ec5-95cc-9d798666d4e0",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "9098f7611b454f28588d6a9faeeb7da5"
        },
        {
          "body": "E makes two sweeping claims: science can do only what is natural, and nothing natural is unethical. The scientist relies on neither. Negate E, say, science can do some things that aren't natural, and the comparison between selective breeding and genetic engineering still stands.",
          "id": "7ca72712-983a-4b7c-9943-b462ce4f7f8c",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "d1a5bc72f7c59caf73342abd72b9ac71"
        }
      ],
      "qid": "PT119-S3-Q20",
      "step_count": 4
    },
