      "explanation_id": "96bfd635-52ff-4f78-818a-12a21aff9e17",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "\"Never unethical\" is a sweeping claim about every imaginable case. The argument does not need that. It just needs the specific kind of manipulation involved in genetic engineering (and the selective-breeding precedent) to be okay. If you negate this and admit that some genetic manipulation is unethical, the scientist can still argue that *this* kind is fine.",
          "id": "3ba12b7a-9f21-45e1-9736-b443222dabb9",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "e0732cc9db3490755f2c8ff6c3d55868"
        },
        {
          "body": "This answer puts the argument on a different foundation — \"if nature does it, science can do it.\" But the scientist is not arguing from nature; the scientist is arguing from a long-standing human practice (selective breeding). The argument can succeed without this principle, so it is not assumed.",
          "id": "efa408ae-bff9-4f43-88ca-4ae0841b9787",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "337e92aa06a23f37241c7535a03a5c1c"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the assumption the argument cannot do without. The scientist's entire defense is: \"Selective breeding is genetic engineering, we have been doing it for millennia, so genetic engineering is not unethical.\"\n\nThat move only works if selective breeding itself is not unethical. If you negate this answer — say, \"selective breeding actually is unethical\" — then \"we have been doing it for millennia\" no longer saves anything. It just means humans have been doing something unethical for a long time. The conclusion collapses.\n\nThat negation test (\"argument falls apart when negated\") is exactly the test for a necessary assumption.",
          "id": "d83185c8-252a-4b79-87ce-73bcc0736bcb",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "23f52b0864be967c89eb6dbfa9d464eb"
        },
        {
          "body": "The argument is purely about ethics, not importance. Even if selective breeding turned out to be totally unimportant for human survival, the scientist's argument still works the same way: it is a long-standing practice that, if ethical, transfers ethical cover to genetic engineering. Importance does not enter the picture.",
          "id": "ecb68f47-a61f-482f-934a-8b58e6b002e7",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "c74d9db2582864711e7d1feadd465f8f"
        },
        {
          "body": "This answer makes a sweeping claim about nature and what science can do. The scientist's argument is much narrower — it just needs selective breeding to be acceptable. The argument can succeed without this universal principle, so it is not necessary.",
          "id": "dd5b73e6-d1a5-4dac-b9fc-9312fe5871f3",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "927942be495219aa48e8e899938a6b19"
        }
      ],
