    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Out of Scope",
          "text": "How much more salt than is contained in a rat’s normal diet was there in the high-salt diet?"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Out of Scope",
          "text": "Did the high blood pressure have any adverse health effects on those rats that developed it?"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Out of Scope",
          "text": "What percentage of naturally occurring rat colonies feed on high-salt diets?"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "How many rats in the colony studied had abnormally high blood pressure before the study began?"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Out of Scope",
          "text": "Have other species of rodents been used in experiments of the same kind?"
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "fac5fed9-caa3-409d-a5c3-44b496727007",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "Imagine two answers: twice the normal salt, or ten times. Either way, we still don't know whether the rats had high blood pressure before the study. The amount of salt tells us how big the dose was, not whether the dose did anything.",
          "id": "5201a04c-8111-4353-93d9-b6599f6c1a52",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "cdea0e18bf72c086cac0e0c68924a20b"
        },
        {
          "body": "Suppose the high blood pressure caused serious harm, or none at all. The conclusion is only that high-salt diets are linked to high blood pressure. What high blood pressure does afterward can't tell us whether salt produced it.",
          "id": "cb25f9bc-5c54-402b-b304-cef5fa0449e6",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "c45874c1516db1684ddf5a296f9a02ba"
        },
        {
          "body": "How many wild colonies eat salty food tells us how common the diet is, not what it does. Whether the answer is 1 percent or 90 percent, this study's evidence for a link is exactly as strong as before.",
          "id": "ea79abb1-e1f7-4376-b6f9-6235bf63eb65",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "8bf032c5aaf508b1dbaab6ee9d9c6374"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the before-measurement the study is missing. Run both answers. If most of the colony already had abnormally high blood pressure, the after-numbers show nothing about salt and the conclusion falls apart. If almost none did, a jump to 75 percent points straight at the diet.\n\nOne question, two answers, opposite effects on the conclusion: that's what a relevant Evaluate question does. Next time a study reports only results after a treatment, ask what the numbers were before it.",
          "id": "64884a51-d794-4886-84ae-ed3ef63e7e32",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "5118a4129db2dc9e70b9e4f81b6c2d14"
        },
        {
          "body": "Yes or no, other rodent experiments say nothing about this colony. Even a yes wouldn't tell us what those experiments found, and the conclusion is about rats anyway.",
          "id": "834a2eeb-c931-4942-b72b-43979984981e",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "102602f0b8de0a791edd917f4422f415"
        }
      ],
