        },
        {
          "body": "This is the question that has to be asked. The whole argument turns on whether the diet actually changed the rats' blood pressure or whether the rats already had high blood pressure to begin with.\n\nImagine the answer: \"Most of the rats had high blood pressure before the study.\" Now the salt diet has not been shown to cause anything; the high blood pressure was already there.\n\nNow imagine the opposite: \"Almost none of the rats had high blood pressure before the study.\" Now the diet looks like it really did something — three-quarters of them developed high blood pressure during the study. The whole conclusion swings on this answer.",
          "id": "e9ebd47d-cd18-4362-823b-adf8fdaeb0f7",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "1f7e4492077c1e3cf4704962d34a5703"
        },
        {
          "body": "The conclusion is about rats — not rodents in general. Whether mice or hamsters have been studied does not touch the central question of whether *these* rats already had high blood pressure when the experiment began.",
          "id": "176cb4e7-776c-4b63-a392-ae74fa4a6d93",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "96757b43ab76678a2339ad67b210c991"
        }
      ],
