      "explanation_id": "0bb1aa2f-4156-4210-a078-b02f0ed1113a",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "The ad never mentions customers, repeat or otherwise. Its only evidence is pride and winning. A Method answer has to describe what the argument actually does, and nothing in the ad demonstrates anything about who comes back to buy.",
          "id": "8ed8eaeb-3096-4036-9bc2-7f47c8dce089",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "1ec0dc40b951a81790f0e07f5ef7afed"
        },
        {
          "body": "Check both halves. Conclusion: \"superior to its competitors\" matches \"produce more winning automobiles than our competitors.\" Evidence: \"using an analogy\" matches the Austin Stables comparison. Pride made Austin beat other stables; Barr has similar pride; so Barr will beat other car makers.\n\nWhen an argument borrows a result from one situation and claims it for a similar one, expect the Method answer to say analogy.",
          "id": "b236d999-2cb4-48ed-9547-8f27b285fb86",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "71d58311ec802446e0809e89f78b5a79"
        },
        {
          "body": "C names something the ad does say: Barr has shown pride \"For generations.\" But the ad only asserts it; nothing in the ad proves it. And it is a premise, not where the ad is going. The ad's point is that Barr will out-win its competitors. Asserting a premise and proving a claim are different moves, and a Method answer has to describe the argument's actual work.",
          "id": "7a1208fc-a4a5-494b-ba47-d72b07c207be",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "29b9fee9066731ed174bd12e679952cc"
        },
        {
          "body": "The ad credits pride fully: pride \"accounts for\" Austin's success. It plays pride's role up, not down. Understating it would undercut the ad's own analogy, since Barr's case rests on pride being what makes the difference.",
          "id": "ed458bee-4c9e-4d4b-901d-9b7d0d73d625",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "8744f8eaee16c15fabfc49df9f389b3b"
        },
        {
          "body": "The ad never compares the ages of the two traditions. It says Barr has shown pride \"For generations\" and calls Austin's pride a tradition, but it never says which is older. The analogy needs the pride to be similar, not older.",
          "id": "1ddd3869-909c-4414-80fa-6801deaaa9c4",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "79436b823e72f9819a5c348847cc3490"
        }
      ],
