    {
      "choices": [
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "A",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "takes for granted that there is a causal connection between the hippocampus and being in a state of profound creativity"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "B",
          "tag": "Bad Objection",
          "text": "fails to consider that music is not necessary for one to be in a state of profound creativity"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "C",
          "tag": "Bad Objection",
          "text": "does not rule out the possibility that listening to music by means other than a tape recording also increases theta waves"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": true,
          "letter": "D",
          "tag": "Correct",
          "text": "ignores the possibility that an increase in theta waves may not always be accompanied by a state of profound creativity"
        },
        {
          "is_correct": false,
          "letter": "E",
          "tag": "Bad Description",
          "text": "provides insufficient reasons to believe that people who are not in states of profound creativity have low levels of theta brain waves"
        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "953f9b2b-cb76-40a5-a65a-3c3f44ba133f",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "The hippocampus is just one of the regions where theta waves occur. The argument never needs the hippocampus to cause anything; its leap is from theta waves to creativity. Delete the hippocampus from the stimulus and the flaw is still there.",
          "id": "78949977-e5e9-4cb9-8dab-2783c28b1b08",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "717cce761f26d9c87d68ca89fe5e6989"
        },
        {
          "body": "The columnist says music is *enough* for creativity, not that it is *required*. Pointing out that people can be creative without music agrees with everything the columnist said.",
          "id": "31e3b49d-76e0-4e6f-ad38-0c0faee59866",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "5553e1eb1908cbcc64686d7d3e328d0b"
        },
        {
          "body": "If live music or a phone speaker also raises theta waves, that is perfectly consistent with the argument. It would only widen the conclusion, not undermine it.",
          "id": "1920313a-662a-47d6-982b-4d00b92a09f5",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "ad2e87dd2a6d06f890c02880e0f7810a"
        },
        {
          "body": "The rule is creativity → more theta waves. The columnist runs it backward: more theta waves → creativity. Ask, \"Could theta waves rise without creativity showing up?\" If yes, music can raise theta waves and leave you no more creative than before. A fever comes with a rising temperature, but warming up in a sauna does not give you a fever.",
          "id": "16bddf47-5d12-4baf-9b4b-41e0ece86400",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "c0fd661429daa5b7eb6391fad1214cde"
        },
        {
          "body": "This one is close, so check the wording. The evidence and conclusion are about an *increase* in theta waves. (E) is about people having *low levels* of them. Someone could start with moderate theta levels, get an increase from music, and still not be creative. The gap is whether an increase guarantees creativity, which (D) states directly; a claim about baseline levels does not capture it.",
          "id": "14994764-6347-4804-88ea-aa217dfb794c",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "1d9dba8caedc28104d34a4f750a86359"
        }
      ],
