        }
      ],
      "explanation_id": "6a6a5f87-7508-4b44-b9d4-9cf861b94c26",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "This connects practice to automatic handwriting. That just tells us how kids became automatic. Composition never appears, so it does nothing to show that automatic writing frees up mental resources for other activities.",
          "id": "868d95f4-cdb2-498f-a353-afb2032be832",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "a38901566dcbba7431c6a4b4547192d5"
        },
        {
          "body": "This one tempts because it links automatic writing with composition gains. But it is about kids who were already automatic before the lessons, and that fits both stories equally well: automatic writers had attention free, or they were simply stronger students who would improve at composition anyway. An answer that fits the rival as well as the author's story can't favor the author. B never shows that becoming more automatic did anything.",
          "id": "8de26de0-5100-4c55-a58d-78fe0c824b6b",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "4c822df8d3ba8bea0c81c0ece68466dc"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the prediction. The evidence only said the best improvers ended up most automatic. C says the kids whose automaticity improved more also generally improved more at composition. Now the gain in one skill tracks the gain in the other during the same lessons, which is what the author's story predicts and what the stronger-students story doesn't need.\n\nThe move: when a study's evidence compares a starting trait with an improvement, look for an answer that turns it into change matching change.",
          "id": "3624e49f-9063-49d2-8fcb-50ed4271de99",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "68c9e977a4d8fa3334f03f0e49fc2db5"
        },
        {
          "body": "A representative group helps us apply the results to first-graders in general. It doesn't touch the real question, which is whether automatic handwriting causes the composition gains or something else explains both.",
          "id": "c418baf9-e59b-4f87-ba57-e884d04fed75",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "63a4a4e8b11aec04f814c214c2519105"
        },
        {
          "body": "This runs the other way. Kids who started with strong composition skills became much more automatic, which hints that composition skill (or general ability) drives automaticity. That's a rival story, not support for the author's one.",
          "id": "534f119d-af5b-4035-8d4c-025fc5b19211",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "51fb32f31242cdbeea9cf88a5dd69afe"
        }
      ],
